A||||8||V8Dreamer||Anyone have any tricks to tightening a 2.8 alt belt by yourself?||||1||
Z||000000||V8Dreamer||12-20-2001||02:32 PM||||Well, usually I have someone around to help apply tension when I need to tighten the alt belt, but as of now I don't.  I figured someone out there has probably found a great way to tighten the alt belt on a 2.8 all alone, and quickly too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  So, anyone wanna share any tricks to doing it without a helper?<P>TIA,<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||12-20-2001||02:44 PM||||No real trick to it.  Get under the car, and pull the alt. tight with one hand while you tighten the adjusting bolt with your free hand.  Not exactly easy, but doable.  Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||tesmith66||12-20-2001||02:45 PM||||From below, use a long crowbar and pry it rearward with your foot and tighten the bolts.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||12-20-2001||03:15 PM||||Buy the $8 fan belt tightening tool from J.C. Whitney. My son does his belt on a regular basis- alone.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric_PA||12-20-2001||07:53 PM||||V8dreamer... you may have heard from Rockcrawl that I cary around a 13 mm wrench to fix the alt belt. Well not anymore, I got rid of that darn 2.8. This is what I would do, first off, loosen the bolt back up and tighten just enough so I can pry on the alt, but yet doesn't allow for it to move back, then I go back under the car and tighten it down. I have had to do it many times on the road and got tired of it. It's not the best way to do it, but will get you home. Also get yourself a good belt from the dealer, the ones from autozone I had no luck with. It's time for you to get one of those 4.9's in there.<P>later,<BR>Eric||||reg||1
Z||000005||V8Dreamer||12-21-2001||09:11 AM||||Thx a lot everyone.  Eric - no 4.9 for me man, I've drifted into the 3800SC II crowd....hopefully by summer (or the end of summer) I'll have my new engine in there.  Then I won't even have to worry about the non auto tensioning belt!!!<P>-Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroSTETZ||12-21-2001||10:53 AM||||i get under the car, put a fiolded up towel on my head, brace it against a corbar (which is prying the alt. tight) and push w/ my feet towards the front, then while holding it tight, tighten the bolt. works great, only have to tighten every 6 mos. or so||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||12-21-2001||11:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>Buy the $8 fan belt tightening tool from J.C. Whitney...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>...also available from Harbor Fright store  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, if you are lucky enough to have one of those in your area.  No mail order delay.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000008||V8Dreamer||12-26-2001||04:46 PM||||***UPDATE***<P>I ended up finding a pretty useful "car on the ground" technique using the spare tire pry lug wrench/prybar and jack along with a 1/4" ratchet with 13mm socket and a 13mm wrench.  I used the ratchet to loosen the lower alt belt and the wrench on the upper one....then I used the prybar to apply tension on the belt and placed the jack between the bar and the trunk wall.  I then cranked the jack just enough so that there was sufficient tension on the belt and then used the ratchet to tighten followed by the wrench to make sure it was good and tight(a deep offset 13mm wrench would prolly work great  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  Hopefully this will help somebody out there in a similar situation as me (on the ground with no helper).  If you try this method, be careful not to damage the trunk wall, it is pretty easy to apply A LOT of force with a jack and not even know it!<P>Thanks for the time and space,<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
